=[tomet-parser]

==[tomet-parser]

The recursive-descent parser (`&str -> Document / Value`) and authoritative
source of truth for the Tomet grammar.

==[Architecture & Responsibilities]

- ("Deterministic Static Parser Boundary")
  - `tomet-parser` is strictly a pure, side-effect-free, deterministic static parser.
It performs zero I/O, external file resolution, or dynamic code execution.
Given identical input text, it produces identical AST output with guaranteed
linear/predictable time complexity.
- ("Documents And Patterns")
  - `parse_document` reads an ordinary document, where `?` is text.
`parse_pattern` reads the same grammar with one more sigil, `?`, which makes a
hole (see [`tomet_ast::Hole`]). The parser only builds the hole; what a matcher is and
what a hole matches belongs to the caller (tandr), never to this crate.
- ("Tree-sitter Synchronization")
  - `tomet-parser` is the single source of truth for grammar definitions.
`tree-sitter-tomet` is a separate hand-maintained grammar approximation used for
editor syntax highlighting.

==[Module Layout]

- (dialect) Which sigils may open an element: `@` always, `?` (a hole) only in a pattern.

`parse_document` and `parse_pattern` share every other function; each takes the dialect.

- (cst) The one reader of the grammar's shape: a lossless, resilient Concrete Syntax Tree

, powered by `tomet-cst`.
A blank line or a line-start `;` breaks a block here.

- (lower) The CST read as an AST: `parse_document` and `parse_pattern` are `parse_cst` and

then this. It builds the typed tree and refuses what a document may not say.

- (element) What makes `@` start an element, which characters open a group, and the readers

of a group's values: `(args)`, `{value}`, `#(id)`.

- (value) The Tomet data grammar (maps, sequences, scalars, comments), from `cotomet-syntax`,

with a hook that finds what a value embeds -- an element, `[content]`, a `|` run -- in the CST.

- (inline) What the CST and lowering share about running text: `Text`/`SoftBreak` items, the

probes for formatting delimiters (`*em*`, `**strong**`, `==mark==`) and autolinks.

- (interp) `${...}` interpolation expressions (identifiers, member chains, calls, literals).
- (error) Parse errors: a message plus a byte offset, with line and column resolved once at the entry points (`located_in`).

==[Module cst]

Resilient, lossless Concrete Syntax Tree (CST) parser for Tomet.

Preserves 100% of source characters including trivia (whitespace, newlines, comments).

This is the one reader of the grammar's shape -- where a block, an element, a
group starts and ends. `parse_document` is this tree lowered into the AST
(see `lower.rs`), so a grammar change is made here once. The parser itself
never fails: broken input still gets a tree, and the errors a document is
refused with are found when the tree is lowered.

```text
ROOT
  SECTION                    `=[ T ]` and everything up to the next section
    SECTION_HEADING          of the same or a shallower level (they nest)
    PARAGRAPH | LIST | BLOCK_ELEMENT | CODE_BLOCK | THEMATIC_BREAK | SECTION ...
```

An element (`BLOCK_ELEMENT`, `INLINE_ELEMENT`) is a `SIGIL` followed by
its groups -- `ARGS` `( )`, `VALUE_DATA` `{ }`, `CONTENT` `[ ]`, at most
one of each -- then any `CONNECT` (`:name(...)`).
`${ ... }` is an `INTERP_EXPR`. Inside `ARGS`/`VALUE_DATA` an entry is a
`MAP_ENTRY` (`key: value`) or a `SEQ_ITEM`, and nested groups are
`MAP_LITERAL`, `CONTENT` (a value written `[ ... ]` is blocks, as in the AST
parser; the list literal is gone) or (a call such as `list(...)`) `ARGS`.
`CONTENT` recurses into the same block grammar `ROOT`/`SECTION` use
(`SECTION`, `LIST`, `BLOCK_ELEMENT`, `CODE_BLOCK`, `THEMATIC_BREAK`,
`PARAGRAPH`), bounded by the group's own closing `]` instead of EOF or
a shallower section -- a block nested inside `[content]` (and its own
`#(id)`) is represented, not flattened into inline text.

Running text carries `EMPHASIS`, `STRONG`, `STRIKEOUT`, `CODE_SPAN` and
`AUTOLINK` nodes where the AST has an `em`, `strong`, `strikeout`,
`raw` or `link` element. What opens a span, and where an address ends, is
decided by the same functions in `inline.rs`, so the two cannot drift.
The tokenizer reads text without context (`_em_` is one identifier, a `//`
inside backticks starts a comment), so a token is split where such a span
starts or ends: the tree is the same text, cut at other places.

A `|` run is `[content]` spelled without brackets, and so it is the same
node: a `CONTENT` that opens with a `PIPE` and has no closing `]`. It is an
element's content (`@quote| text`, or `|` on the line after the element), a
list item's (`-| text`), a heading's (`=| text`) or a `key: | prose` value.
It ends at the first line that does not repeat its marker in the same column
-- a blank line, or a `|` somewhere else, ends it too -- and the error for that
last case (a marker in a column that continues nothing) is lowering's, not the tree's. The `|` that begins each later
line is a `PIPE` token among the content's own, but the grammar reads it as
white space, so the blocks of a run are the blocks the bracketed form has.

===[Functions]

====[fn parse_cst]

Parse `src` into a lossless, resilient [`SyntaxNode`].

Guaranteed Invariant: `parse_cst(src).text().to_string() == src`.

====[fn parse_cst_pattern]

[`parse_cst`] for a pattern: `?matcher(...)` is a hole, an element of its own
kind, where in a document `?` is text.

==[Functions]

===[fn parse_document]

Parse an entire source string as a markup [`tomet_ast::Document`]: the CST of
the text ([`parse_cst`]), read as the AST it stands for.

===[fn parse_pattern]

Parse an entire source string as a pattern: a [`tomet_ast::Document`] in which
`?name(args){options}[content]` is a hole ([`tomet_ast::Hole`]).

It is [`parse_document`] with one more sigil. Everything else is read by the same code, so a
pattern is written in, and printed as, ordinary tomet.

===[fn parse_interpolation_str]

Parse a `$func(args)` or `${expr}` interpolation directly from a string slice.
